    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat from inside to outside, using a special fluid called refrigerant. First, the refrigerant flows through pipes inside the fridge and absorbs heat from your food, turning into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, which makes it hot. Next, the hot gas travels through coils on the back or bottom of the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it flows through a tiny valve that makes it cold again, and the cycle repeats. So a fridge doesn't really \"make cold\"—it just keeps pumping heat out, keeping your food chilly."
  }
